WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.120
We now finish the general argument.
Node seven gave an alternating sum,

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.740
script S, congruent to
zero modulo one plus i.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:13.060
Node five gave E of P congruent to
the all ones polynomial, J of P.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.520
We will evaluate that
same sum a second way.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:22.880
Let I contain the distinct primes dividing
our odd integer u, greater than one.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.980
Each subset S supplies the product
of its chosen primitive roots.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.370
The empty subset gives one.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:35.900
Every nonempty subset gives a nontrivial
root whose order divides u squared.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:42.280
Therefore the u squared terms of J
cancel exactly at every nonempty input.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:48.180
At one, they add to u squared. These are
exact geometric sums, before reduction.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.620
Now reduce modulo one plus i.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:57.580
We may replace each E evaluation
by its corresponding J evaluation.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.680
The denominators stay invertible:

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:05.820
the norm identity supplies their inverses
after inverting the odd number u.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:10.420
Thus a zero numerator really
does give a zero term.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.040
Every nonempty subset disappears,
leaving only the empty subset.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:19.780
The surviving term is J of
one divided by c of one.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:25.000
Since c of one is positive or negative u,
this quotient is positive or negative u.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:30.060
The integer u is odd. Pairs of
ones vanish modulo one plus i,

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.280
leaving one; a negative
sign has the same residue.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:38.860
So script S is congruent to one.
Compare the two conclusions.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:41.590
Node seven gives residue zero,

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:46.000
while the all ones polynomial
gives residue one for the same sum.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:50.480
Our reduction ring is nonzero,
so zero and one are different.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:55.400
This contradiction rules out
every odd u greater than one.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:57.660
Return to the order restriction.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.980
An order n greater than one must
equal four u squared, with u odd.

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:06.170
We have eliminated every
u greater than one.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:11.020
The only remaining value is u
equal to one, giving order four.

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:15.920
Order one is the separate case.
Both remaining orders occur.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:20.300
At order one, use the one by
one matrix with entry one.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:26.180
At order four, use our circulant example
whose first row is negative one, one,

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:32.110
one, one. Its rows are mutually
orthogonal and have squared length four.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:36.760
The argument concludes that a real
circulant Hadamard matrix exists exactly

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:40.920
at orders one and four. This
completes the eight step proof map.
